<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">BBC</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as British Broadcasting Corporation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The BBC, the British Broadcasting Corporation, is the United Kingdom's public broadcaster, founded as a company in 1922 and established by royal charter in 1927. Funded mainly by a licence fee, it became a model of public-service broadcasting. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its broadcasts to occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>'s appeal of 18 June 1940, made it a voice of resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bbc.jpg" alt="BBC" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suicasmo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Broadcasting_House_20160816.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s first live public broadcast was made from the Marconi Company's factory in Chelmsford in June 1920. Sponsored by the Daily Mail's Alfred Harmsworth, 1st Viscount Northcliffe, it featured the Australian soprano Dame Nellie Melba and caught the public imagination. Official circles did not share the enthusiasm, since such broadcasts were held to interfere with military and civil communications, and by late 1920 the General Post Office had secured a ban on further Chelmsford broadcasts. The British Broadcasting Company was formed on 18 October 1922.</p><p>Its finances soon proved inadequate, and by mid-1923 talks with the Post Office were deadlocked. The Sykes Committee recommended a simple 10 shilling licence fee to fund broadcasts, and a monopoly without advertising. To avoid competition with newspapers, Fleet Street persuaded the government to ban news bulletins before 7 pm. In September 1923 John Reith launched the Radio Times, the first broadcast listings magazine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The British Broadcasting Corporation came into existence on 1 January 1927, with the newly knighted Reith as its first director general. It adopted the motto Nation shall speak peace unto Nation, and the World Service was launched in 1932 as the Empire Service. From as early as the 1930s until the 1990s the security service MI5 vetted applicants for BBC jobs without their knowledge, an arrangement that began informally in 1933 and was formalised from 1935; the BBC denied any suggestion of it.</p><p>Television broadcasting was suspended from 1 September 1939 to 7 June 1946, and radio kept the nation's spirits up. Most radio operations moved out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> and then to Bedford, where concerts were broadcast from the Corn Exchange. In 1941 the Archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> and York broadcast from St Paul's Church there on the National Day of Prayer. George Orwell worked for the BBC for two years during the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The BBC is a chartered corporation, independent of direct government intervention. Its royal charter sets out its mission and public purposes, emphasises public service and limited editorial independence, and prohibits advertising on domestic services. It is funded principally by the television licence fee, set by the government and enforced by the criminal law. Its headquarters, Broadcasting House in Portland Place, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, carries statues of Prospero and Ariel by Eric Gill. The BBC has often been criticised over its impartiality, with accusations of bias made from both left and right.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “BBC” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under John Reith it became the public <b>British Broadcasting Corporation</b> in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
